ML_PAGE("l5-prep", "<header class=\"page-header theme-l5\"><span class=\"eyebrow\">CHAPTER 5 · MCQ PREPARATION</span><h2>Chapter 5 MCQ Prep</h2><p>The complete MCQ preparation notes in their original order: 40 multiple-choice questions sit inside the sections they belong to, with the answer, the logic and the exam trap hidden until you reveal them.</p></header><div class=\"study-grid three-col\"><div class=\"metric-card\"><strong>40</strong><span>MCQs in the notes</span></div><div class=\"metric-card\"><strong>40</strong><span>Exam traps explained</span></div><div class=\"metric-card\"><strong>30</strong><span>Difficult-mode questions</span></div></div><div class=\"action-row\"><button class=\"primary-btn bg-l5\" data-quiz=\"5\">Start Chapter 5 simulator</button></div><details class=\"topic-index toc-panel theme-l5\" id=\"l5p-toc\"><summary><strong>MCQ notes</strong><span class=\"toc-count\">7 sections</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><input type=\"search\" class=\"toc-search\" placeholder=\"Filter sections...\" aria-label=\"Filter sections\"><ul class=\"toc-list\"><li class=\"toc-group\">PART A - EXAM STRATEGY</li><li data-full=\"Your practice examination\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5p-s2\" title=\"Your practice examination\">Your practice examination</button></li><li data-full=\"Three reasoning principles before you begin\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5p-s3\" title=\"Three reasoning principles before you begin\">Three reasoning principles before you begin</button></li><li class=\"toc-group\">PART B - PORTFOLIO CONSTRUCTION QUESTIONS</li><li data-full=\"Section A - Portfolio Construction &amp; Diversification (Q1-Q20)\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5p-s5\" title=\"Section A - Portfolio Construction &amp; Diversification (Q1-Q20)\">Section A: portfolios (Q1-Q20)</button></li><li class=\"toc-group\">PART C - BETA, CAPM AND MPT QUESTIONS</li><li data-full=\"Section B - Beta, CAPM &amp; Portfolio Optimization (Q21-Q40)\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5p-s7\" title=\"Section B - Beta, CAPM &amp; Portfolio Optimization (Q21-Q40)\">Section B: beta and CAPM (Q21-Q40)</button></li><li class=\"toc-group\">PART D - FORMULAS AND REVIEW</li><li data-full=\"Chapter 5 formula sheet\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5p-s9\" title=\"Chapter 5 formula sheet\">Chapter 5 formula sheet</button></li><li data-full=\"How to diagnose your exam mistakes\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5p-s10\" title=\"How to diagnose your exam mistakes\">How to diagnose your exam mistakes</button></li><li data-full=\"The highest-level reasoning to master\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l5p-s11\" title=\"The highest-level reasoning to master\">The highest-level reasoning to master</button></li></ul></details><details class=\"prep-notes theme-l5\" open><summary><div><strong>Chapter 5 MCQ preparation</strong><small>Exam strategy, practice questions, formula sheets and review - section by section, answers hidden.</small></div><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"markdown-body compact\"><div class=\"notes-toolbar\"><button type=\"button\" data-sections=\"open\">Expand all</button><button type=\"button\" data-sections=\"close\">Collapse all</button><span>7 sections · click a heading to open it</span></div><h2>Chapter 5 - MCQ Exam Preparation</h2>\n<p>Fundamentals of Investing · Modern Portfolio Concepts</p>\n<p>Chapter 5 is particularly suitable for difficult MCQs because the correct answer often contradicts common intuition.</p>\n<p>For example, adding a highly volatile stock can actually reduce portfolio risk. Similarly, a stock with a lower beta is not necessarily less volatile overall.</p>\n<p>This practice exam is based on your uploaded Chapter 5 slides, covering portfolio returns, standard deviation, correlation, international diversification, systematic risk, beta, CAPM, the Security Market Line, the efficient frontier, and traditional versus modern portfolio management.</p><h2 class=\"notes-divider\" id=\"l5p-d1\">PART A - EXAM STRATEGY</h2><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5p-s2\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Your practice examination</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p><strong>Questions:</strong> 40 · <strong>Suggested time:</strong> 60 min · <strong>Target score:</strong> 80%</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Section A - Portfolio Construction &amp; Diversification</strong> (20 questions): Portfolio returns, variance, correlations, asset combinations, international diversification and risk decomposition.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Section B - Beta, CAPM &amp; Portfolio Optimization</strong> (20 questions): Market sensitivity, portfolio beta, required return, SML, efficient frontier and investment decisions.</p>\n</li>\n</ul></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5p-s3\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Three reasoning principles before you begin</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><ol>\n<li>\n<p>Individual risk is not portfolio risk. A security's contribution depends on its portfolio weight, volatility and correlations with the other investments.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Total volatility is not systematic risk. Standard deviation measures total return variation, whereas beta measures sensitivity to market movements.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>A higher return is not automatically more attractive. You must evaluate whether it justifies the risk and whether another feasible portfolio offers a superior risk-return trade-off.</p>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The test progresses from applications to quantitative analysis and evaluations. Assume no short selling, leverage, transaction costs or other constraints unless a question states otherwise.</p></div></details><h2 class=\"notes-divider\" id=\"l5p-d4\">PART B - PORTFOLIO CONSTRUCTION QUESTIONS</h2><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5p-s5\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Section A - Portfolio Construction &amp; Diversification (Q1-Q20)</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q1</span><div><p>An investor's primary objective is to receive regular dividend and interest payments rather than maximize long-term share-price appreciation. Which portfolio type best matches the chapter?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Growth-oriented portfolio</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Income-oriented portfolio</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Speculative portfolio concentrated in derivatives</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Portfolio selected solely by historical beta</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>The chapter distinguishes income-oriented portfolios, which emphasize regular interest and dividend income, from growth-oriented portfolios, which emphasize long-term capital appreciation.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Portfolio objectives describe the investor's intended benefits, not simply the number of securities owned.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q2</span><div><p>Portfolio A has an expected return of 8% and standard deviation of 12%. Portfolio B has an expected return of 10% and the same standard deviation. Under the chapter's mean-variance framework, which statement is correct?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> A is superior because it has lower return.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Both are equally efficient because their risks are identical.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> B dominates A because it offers more expected return for the same measured risk.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> A must be preferred by every risk-averse investor.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>An efficient portfolio offers the highest expected return for a given level of risk. Since B provides more expected return with identical standard deviation, A is dominated on these measures.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Risk aversion does not justify selecting a lower-return portfolio when another portfolio has exactly the same measured risk.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q3</span><div><p>An investor allocates 50% to Stock A with an expected return of 12%, 30% to Stock B at 8%, and 20% to bonds at 5%. What is the portfolio's expected return?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 9.4%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 8.33%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 12.0%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 8.0%</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>E(Rp) = 0.50(12%) + 0.30(8%) + 0.20(5%) = 6% + 2.4% + 1% = 9.4%. Portfolio expected return is a weighted average of constituent expected returns.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Apply portfolio weights instead of taking the simple arithmetic mean of individual expected returns.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q4</span><div><p>Stock A has an expected return of 12%, while Stock B has an expected return of 8%. An investor wants a fully invested two-stock portfolio with an expected return of 11%. What proportion must be allocated to A?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 25%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 50%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 60%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 75%</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Let w be Stock A's weight. Then 11% = 12%w + 8%(1 - w), so 3% = 4%w and w = 0.75. The portfolio therefore requires 75% in A and 25% in B.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Portfolio weights must add to 100%; solve for the unknown weight rather than averaging the returns.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q5</span><div><p>Why is portfolio standard deviation generally not equal to the weighted average of individual stock standard deviations?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Because portfolio weights cannot be expressed as percentages.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Because portfolio risk also depends on how asset returns move together.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Because all portfolios have a standard deviation of zero.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Because diversification necessarily raises expected returns.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>The chapter explicitly states that portfolio standard deviation depends on weights, individual standard deviations and correlations. Correlation determines whether individual price movements reinforce or offset one another.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Expected return is a weighted average; portfolio standard deviation generally is not.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q6</span><div><p>Two securities have a return correlation coefficient of -0.80. Which interpretation is most accurate?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Their returns tend to move in opposite directions strongly.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Their returns must be identical.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Both securities must generate negative expected returns.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Their portfolio weights are automatically equal.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>A strongly negative correlation indicates that the two return series tend to move in opposite directions. Correlation measures co-movement, not average return, allocation or guaranteed performance.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Negative correlation does not mean negative investment returns.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q7</span><div><p>Two assets have standard deviations of 20% and 10%, respectively. They are equally weighted and perfectly positively correlated (ρ = +1). What is the portfolio standard deviation?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 10%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 11.18%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 15%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 30%</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>With perfect positive correlation and nonnegative weights, portfolio standard deviation equals 0.5(20%) + 0.5(10%) = 15%. No diversification reduction occurs relative to the weighted average in this special case.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> The weighted-average risk shortcut works here because correlation equals +1, not because the portfolio has two assets.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q8</span><div><p>Two equally weighted assets have standard deviations of 20% and 10%, with zero correlation. What is the portfolio's approximate standard deviation?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 15.00%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 5.00%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 10.00%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 11.18%</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>σp = sqrt[(0.5² x 0.20²) + (0.5² x 0.10²)] = sqrt(0.0125) = 0.1118, or 11.18%. The covariance term is zero because the correlation coefficient equals zero.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Zero correlation does not mean zero portfolio risk.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q9</span><div><p>Two equally weighted assets have standard deviations of 20% and 10%. Their correlation is -0.50. What is the portfolio's approximate standard deviation?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 8.66%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 11.18%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 15.00%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 5.00%</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Portfolio variance = 0.5²(0.20²) + 0.5²(0.10²) + 2(0.5)(0.5)(0.20)(0.10)(-0.5) = 0.0075. Taking the square root gives 8.66%. Negative covariance reduces risk.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> The negative correlation term must be subtracted through the covariance component; do not simply subtract 0.50 from a standard deviation.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q10</span><div><p>Two assets have σA = 20%, σB = 10%, and perfect negative correlation. Assuming only positive portfolio weights and full investment, what weights would eliminate portfolio variance?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 50% A and 50% B</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> One-third A and two-thirds B</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Two-thirds A and one-third B</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 100% A and 0% B</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>With ρ = -1, the risk terms cancel when wAσA = wBσB. Thus 0.20wA = 0.10wB, with wA + wB = 1, giving wA = 1/3 and wB = 2/3. This is the mathematical condition underlying the slide's perfect-negative-correlation principle.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Perfect negative correlation permits complete risk cancellation with appropriate weights, not necessarily with equal weights.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q11</span><div><p>Three otherwise identical two-asset portfolios have return correlations of +0.90, +0.20 and -0.60. Their weights and individual asset volatilities are identical and positive. Which portfolio has the lowest standard deviation?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The portfolio with +0.90 correlation</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The portfolio with +0.20 correlation</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The portfolio with -0.60 correlation</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> All must have the same portfolio volatility</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Holding asset volatilities and positive weights constant, decreasing correlation reduces the covariance component and therefore reduces portfolio variance. The -0.60 portfolio has the lowest risk of the three.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Compare correlation while holding other variables constant; otherwise the conclusion may not follow.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q12</span><div><p>Asset A earns 20% in a strong economy and 0% in a weak economy. Asset B earns 0% in a strong economy and 20% in a weak economy. Both states are equally likely. If the assets are equally weighted, what portfolio return occurs in each state?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 20% in both states</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 20% in the strong state and 0% in the weak state</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 0% in both states</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 10% in both states</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>In either state, the portfolio earns 0.5(20%) + 0.5(0%) = 10%. The opposing returns cancel the variation in this simplified two-state example.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> An individually volatile asset can contribute to a stable portfolio when its movements offset those of another asset.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q13</span><div><p>In the chapter's IBM-Celgene example, Celgene has a much higher individual standard deviation than IBM, yet a portfolio holding approximately 86% IBM and 14% Celgene has a lower standard deviation than IBM alone. What does this illustrate?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Celgene must be risk-free.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Adding a highly volatile asset can reduce total portfolio risk when its covariance benefits are sufficient.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Portfolio standard deviation is always equal to the smaller asset's standard deviation.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The higher-returning asset always eliminates all volatility.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Table 5.1 reports approximately 28.6% volatility for IBM, 58.3% for Celgene and 25.3% for their 86/14 portfolio. The portfolio outcome reflects the co-movement of their returns, not just individual volatility.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Evaluate a new asset by its effect on portfolio risk, not solely by its standalone volatility.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q14</span><div><p>In Table 5.3, Asset X returns rise from 8% to 16% while Asset Y's returns fall from 24% to 8%. What is the standard deviation of a portfolio containing two-thirds X and one-third Y across the five scenarios shown?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 0%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 3.2%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 4.2%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 6.3%</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>For every year in Table 5.3, (2/3)RX + (1/3)RY gives approximately 13.33%. Because the portfolio return is constant across the shown scenarios, its standard deviation is zero.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Do not assume that positive individual standard deviations necessarily imply positive portfolio standard deviation.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q15</span><div><p>Table 5.3 reports approximately the same average return for portfolios XY and XZ, but standard deviations of 0% and 4.2%, respectively. Which portfolio dominates under the chapter's stated risk-return measures?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> XZ, because higher risk is always superior.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Both, because mean returns are equal.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> XY, because it provides comparable average return with lower volatility.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Neither can be assessed even under the chapter's two stated metrics.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>XY and XZ have average returns of approximately 13.3%, but XY's standard deviation is zero in the given example while XZ has 4.2%. XY offers the better measured risk-return combination.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A higher risk level is not itself desirable when no additional expected return is provided.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q16</span><div><p>A Malaysian equity portfolio is concentrated in domestic industries. The investor considers adding securities from economies whose business cycles are not perfectly aligned with Malaysia's. What is the strongest justification?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Foreign investments cannot experience recessions.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Owning foreign securities guarantees higher returns.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Currency differences eliminate all investment risks.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Different economic-cycle exposures may provide diversification benefits.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>The chapter emphasizes that foreign markets may not move together with domestic markets, creating diversification opportunities. The benefit depends on return relationships and does not guarantee higher returns or protection from global crises.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> International diversification can reduce some exposures without eliminating common global risk.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q17</span><div><p>A foreign stock rises 10% in its local currency, but its currency depreciates 15% against the investor's home currency. What is the most appropriate interpretation?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The home-currency return must be +10%.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The home-currency return is approximately -6.5%, demonstrating currency exchange risk.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Currency changes cannot affect stock investors.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The home-currency return must be +25%.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>The combined home-currency return is (1.10 x 0.85) - 1 = -0.065, or -6.5%. The chapter identifies currency exchange risk as a disadvantage of international diversification. The numerical combination applies that concept.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Foreign investment performance must be evaluated in the investor's home currency when measuring their actual return.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q18</span><div><p>Which approach is specifically described by the chapter as typically not being considered a true international investment for diversification purposes?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Purchasing shares of a U.S. multinational company with substantial overseas operations</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Investing in a mutual fund that holds foreign company stocks</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Purchasing American Depositary Shares</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Directly holding foreign company equity</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>The chapter notes that investing in U.S. multinational companies is typically not classified as true international investing for diversification purposes, even though multinational businesses may have foreign operations.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A domestic company earning revenue overseas is not structurally identical to directly holding securities of foreign issuers.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q19</span><div><p>One company's share price falls sharply after a lawsuit against its management, while the overall stock market is relatively unaffected. What type of risk is most directly illustrated?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Systematic market risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Interest-rate risk affecting all bonds</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Unsystematic firm-specific risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> A guaranteed risk-free return</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>The chapter identifies lawsuits and labor strikes as examples of unsystematic risk, since they are primarily firm-specific and may be reduced through diversification.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A large loss is not automatically systematic risk; identify whether the cause affects the market broadly or the particular company.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q20</span><div><p>An investor holds shares in 100 companies across multiple industries. An economy-wide financial shock causes most stock prices to decline together. Which conclusion best follows Chapter 5?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The portfolio must be risk-free because it owns many securities.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Only firm-specific risk can explain broad simultaneous declines.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The portfolio was necessarily fraudulent because prices fell.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Diversification cannot fully eliminate systematic risk arising from common market forces.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Diversification can reduce firm-specific unsystematic risk, but systematic risk arises from forces affecting broad groups of investments. The chapter's portfolio-risk diagram shows a residual risk floor as more securities are added.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Many holdings do not imply immunity from broad market conditions.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details><h2 class=\"notes-divider\" id=\"l5p-d6\">PART C - BETA, CAPM AND MPT QUESTIONS</h2><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5p-s7\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Section B - Beta, CAPM &amp; Portfolio Optimization (Q21-Q40)</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q21</span><div><p>What beta value does the chapter assign to the overall market portfolio or its broad market benchmark?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 0</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 1.0</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> -1.0</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> A value that must always exceed 2.0</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>By construction in the chapter's beta framework, the overall market has beta 1.0. Individual securities can have betas above or below one.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A beta of 1 represents market-level sensitivity, not an expected return of 1%.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q22</span><div><p>Stock A has beta 0.5 and total return standard deviation 40%. Stock B has beta 1.3 and total standard deviation 25%. Which statement is most accurate?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> A has greater total volatility, while B has greater market sensitivity.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> A is less volatile in every sense because its beta is smaller.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> B has greater total volatility because its beta exceeds one.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Both securities must have identical systematic risk.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Standard deviation measures overall dispersion of returns, while beta measures exposure to market movements. A has higher standard deviation but lower beta than B.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Low beta is not synonymous with low total standard deviation.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q23</span><div><p>A stock has beta 1.50. In the chapter's simplified market-sensitivity illustration, what market-related return movement corresponds to a 10% increase in the market?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> +10%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> +5%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> +15%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> +1.5%</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>The market-linked component is approximately β x market movement = 1.5 x 10% = 15%. This is the simplified interpretation used in the slides, not a guarantee of the stock's actual realized return.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Beta is market sensitivity, not a promise that the security must produce the calculated return.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q24</span><div><p>A security has beta 0.50. Using the chapter's simplified beta interpretation, what market-related movement corresponds to an 8% market decline?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> -8%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> -16%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> +4%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> -4%</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>The expected market-linked response is approximately 0.50 x (-8%) = -4%. A lower positive beta implies lower sensitivity to the market's return fluctuations.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A beta of 0.5 halves the market-linked movement; it does not reverse its sign.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q25</span><div><p>An asset has a negative beta. Why could it be valuable in a diversified portfolio even if its expected return is relatively low?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Negative beta guarantees that the investment's own price can never fall.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The asset may provide a hedge against some market movements by tending to move oppositely to the market.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Its return is always negative.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> It automatically eliminates all portfolio risk regardless of weight.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Negative beta means the asset's market-related return sensitivity is opposite in sign to the benchmark. This may offer a diversification or hedging benefit, making the asset valuable despite a potentially lower required return.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Negative beta does not mean guaranteed profits, negative expected return or complete portfolio insurance.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q26</span><div><p>Why does the chapter treat systematic risk, rather than total standalone volatility, as the relevant positively rewarded risk within the CAPM framework?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Because standard deviation is mathematically meaningless.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Because every security has zero firm-specific risk.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Because diversified investors can reduce firm-specific risk, leaving market-related risk as the relevant priced component.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Because higher volatility always produces a guaranteed higher realized return.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>The slides describe only nondiversifiable risk as relevant to the positive risk-return relationship under CAPM. The reasoning assumes investors can diversify away much of the firm-specific risk of individual securities.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> CAPM's pricing relationship concerns systematic risk, not guaranteed compensation for every form of uncertainty.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q27</span><div><p>A portfolio holds 40% in a stock with beta 1.5 and 60% in a stock with beta 0.5. What is the portfolio beta?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 0.90</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 1.00</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 1.20</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 0.75</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Portfolio beta is the weighted average of individual betas: βp = 0.40(1.5) + 0.60(0.5) = 0.60 + 0.30 = 0.90.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Portfolio beta is a weighted average even though portfolio standard deviation generally is not.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q28</span><div><p>In Table 5.6, Portfolio V has weights of 10%, 30%, 20%, 20% and 20%, with corresponding betas of 1.65, 1.00, 1.30, 1.10 and 1.25. What is Portfolio V's beta?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 0.910</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 1.500</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 1.000</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 1.195</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>βV = 0.10(1.65) + 0.30(1.00) + 0.20(1.30) + 0.20(1.10) + 0.20(1.25) = 1.195. This uses the portfolio beta formula given in the chapter.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Do not take the unweighted mean of the constituent betas when allocation weights differ.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q29</span><div><p>Table 5.6's Portfolio W has weights 10%, 10%, 20%, 10% and 50% and corresponding betas 0.80, 1.00, 0.65, 0.75 and 1.05. What is the portfolio beta, and how does it compare with Portfolio V's beta of 1.195?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 1.195, identical to V</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 0.910, indicating less market sensitivity than V</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 1.050, indicating more sensitivity than V</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 0.500, meaning it eliminates all market risk</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>βW = 0.10(0.80) + 0.10(1.00) + 0.20(0.65) + 0.10(0.75) + 0.50(1.05) = 0.910. This is lower than V's beta of 1.195, so W has lower market sensitivity.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Compare portfolio betas to assess relative market sensitivity, not necessarily total standard deviation.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q30</span><div><p>The risk-free rate is 4%, expected market return is 10%, and a security has beta 1.5. What is its CAPM-required return?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 10%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 15%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 13%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 9%</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>CAPM required return = Rf + β(E[Rm] - Rf) = 4% + 1.5(10% - 4%) = 4% + 9% = 13%.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Apply beta to the market risk premium, not the entire market return.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q31</span><div><p>The risk-free rate is 3%, expected market return is 11%, and an investment has beta 0.75. What required return does CAPM imply?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 9%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 8.25%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 11%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 6%</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Market risk premium = 11% - 3% = 8%. Required return = 3% + 0.75(8%) = 9%.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A beta of 0.75 indicates 75% of the market risk premium, not 75% of the entire market return.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q32</span><div><p>The risk-free rate is 5%, the expected market return is 9%, and CAPM implies that a security requires 13%. What is its beta?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 1.0</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 1.5</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 0.5</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 2.0</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>13% = 5% + β(9% - 5%). Rearranging gives β = (13% - 5%)/4% = 2.0. Beta can be recovered from the required risk premium and the market risk premium.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> When beta is the unknown, rearrange the CAPM formula rather than guessing from the required return.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q33</span><div><p>The expected market return is 12%, while the risk-free rate is 4%. What is the market risk premium used in CAPM?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 12%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 8%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 16%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 3%</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>The market risk premium is the expected market return in excess of the risk-free rate: 12% - 4% = 8%. It forms the slope of the Security Market Line in the standard CAPM framework.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Market risk premium is not the total market return.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q34</span><div><p>Assume the risk-free rate is 4%, expected market return is 10%, and a security has beta -0.5. What CAPM-required return follows?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 7%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 4%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 1%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> -1%</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Required return = 4% + (-0.5)(10% - 4%) = 4% - 3% = 1%. Negative market exposure can carry a lower required return because of its potential hedging value.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A negative beta does not automatically mean a negative CAPM-required return.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q35</span><div><p>Figure 5.6 shows a 6% risk-free rate and a 10% required return for beta 1.0. According to this SML, what required return corresponds to beta 1.25?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 11%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 12.5%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 10.25%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 13.5%</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>The market risk premium is 10% - 6% = 4%. At beta 1.25, the required return is 6% + 1.25(4%) = 11%. This reproduces the relationship illustrated in Figure 5.6.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Read the SML using its intercept and slope rather than multiplying beta directly by the market return.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q36</span><div><p>In a standard CAPM Security Market Line, the risk-free rate is held constant while the expected market risk premium increases. What happens to the SML?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> It becomes horizontal regardless of beta.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Its vertical intercept becomes zero.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> It shifts downward in parallel.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> It becomes steeper while retaining the same intercept.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>The CAPM line has intercept Rf and slope E(Rm) - Rf. Increasing the market risk premium while keeping Rf fixed increases the slope without changing the beta-zero intercept.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Distinguish changes to the SML intercept from changes to the slope.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q37</span><div><p>An asset has beta 1.2. The risk-free rate is 4% and the market's expected return is 10%. Your analysis estimates an expected return of 13%. Where does the asset lie relative to the SML?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Below the SML by 1.8 percentage points</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Above the SML by 1.8 percentage points</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Exactly on the SML</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Below the SML by 3 percentage points</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>CAPM-required return = 4% + 1.2(6%) = 11.2%. Expected return exceeds this by 13% - 11.2% = 1.8 percentage points, so the asset lies above the SML on these assumptions.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A positive expected alpha is relative to the model and forecast; it is not proof that the estimate will be realized.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q38</span><div><p>Which comparison correctly distinguishes the efficient frontier from the Security Market Line?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Both plot total portfolio standard deviation on their horizontal axes.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The efficient frontier plots beta, while the SML plots the number of securities.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The efficient frontier considers expected return against portfolio standard deviation, while the SML relates required return to beta.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Both graphs demonstrate that return is independent of risk.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>The efficient frontier describes attainable efficient portfolio combinations in expected-return/standard-deviation space. The SML graphs CAPM-required return against systematic risk measured by beta.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A graph's horizontal-axis risk measure is often the easiest way to distinguish these two concepts.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q39</span><div><p>Portfolio A offers 10% expected return at 15% standard deviation. Portfolio B offers 12% expected return at the same 15% standard deviation. Both are feasible under identical assumptions. What should an investor conclude under MPT?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> B dominates A; A is inefficient on the return-volatility comparison.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> A is necessarily efficient because its return is lower.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Both portfolios must be on the efficient frontier.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> B must be rejected because higher return always means higher standard deviation.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>B gives a higher expected return at exactly the same standard deviation. Therefore A is dominated and cannot be efficient under the stated mean-variance opportunity set.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> An investor who wants lower risk should choose a lower-risk efficient portfolio, not a dominated one.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q40</span><div><p>An advisor recommends a portfolio of 30 familiar companies and claims it is well diversified solely because it holds many securities. Which improvement best reconciles traditional portfolio management with MPT as recommended by the chapter?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Choose only the stocks with the highest previous-year returns.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Ignore the existing holdings and buy more companies from the same industry.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Replace every asset with the lowest-beta security regardless of investor objectives.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Evaluate expected returns, asset weights, standard deviations, correlations and portfolio beta, then select an allocation suited to the investor's risk tolerance.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>The chapter's recommended reconciliation combines practical diversification across assets and industries with MPT's statistical analysis. It calls for attention to correlations, portfolio beta, investor risk tolerance and efficient risk-return trade-offs.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Holding many securities is a starting point, not evidence that the resulting portfolio has been efficiently constructed.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details><h2 class=\"notes-divider\" id=\"l5p-d8\">PART D - FORMULAS AND REVIEW</h2><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5p-s9\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Chapter 5 formula sheet</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"l5_sml_sketch\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>Securities above the SML offer more than CAPM requires for their beta; below it, less.</figcaption></figure></div><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Concept</th>\n<th>Formula</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Portfolio expected return</td>\n<td>$E(R_p)=\\sum w_iE(R_i)$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Two-asset variance</td>\n<td>$\\sigma_p^2=w_A^2\\sigma_A^2+w_B^2\\sigma_B^2+2w_Aw_B\\sigma_A\\sigma_B\\rho_{AB}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Portfolio standard deviation</td>\n<td>$\\sigma_p=\\sqrt{\\sigma_p^2}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Correlation</td>\n<td>$-1\\le\\rho_{AB}\\le+1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Portfolio beta</td>\n<td>$\\beta_p=\\sum w_i\\beta_i$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Market risk premium</td>\n<td>$E(R_m)-R_f$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>CAPM-required return</td>\n<td>$R_i=R_f+\\beta_i[E(R_m)-R_f]$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Expected CAPM alpha</td>\n<td>$\\alpha_i=E(R_i)-R_{i,\\mathrm{CAPM}}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The two-asset variance formula, the alpha comparison and the zero-variance weight condition are mathematical applications or extensions of the chapter's concepts, rather than separately stated learning objectives in the slides.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5p-s10\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">How to diagnose your exam mistakes</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Mistake pattern</th>\n<th>What to revisit</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Incorrect portfolio return</td>\n<td>Weighted average, not a simple mean</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Incorrect portfolio standard deviation</td>\n<td>Weights, individual standard deviations and correlation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Confusing correlation and return</td>\n<td>Correlation describes co-movement, not profitability</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Assuming diversification eliminates all risk</td>\n<td>Systematic versus unsystematic risk</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Confusing beta and SD</td>\n<td>Market sensitivity versus total volatility</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Incorrect CAPM calculation</td>\n<td>Beta multiplies the market risk premium</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Incorrect SML interpretation</td>\n<td>Intercept = risk-free rate; slope = market risk premium</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Confusing SML and efficient frontier</td>\n<td>Beta on SML's horizontal axis; portfolio SD on the frontier's</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Assuming 30 stocks guarantee diversification</td>\n<td>Examine correlations, weights and underlying economic exposures</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l5p-s11\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">The highest-level reasoning to master</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Suppose an examiner presents this statement:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"Stock A is much more volatile than Stock B. Therefore, adding Stock A to my portfolio will necessarily increase total portfolio risk.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>A weak answer simply says the statement is false.</p>\n<p>A strong answer identifies the missing information: Stock A's correlation with the existing holdings, its allocation weight and its contribution to portfolio variance. Its high individual standard deviation is insufficient to determine whether portfolio risk rises or falls.</p>\n<p>That is the central intellectual shift in Chapter 5:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Individual Asset Risk}\\neq\\text{Marginal Contribution to Portfolio Risk}}$$</div>\n<p>A particularly effective way to revise after completing the exam is to explain not only why the correct answer works, but which change in assumptions would make another answer correct. That develops the analytical and evaluative judgment needed for harder investment questions.</p>\n<p>Source note: The questions follow the uploaded 2014 Chapter 5 slides. Historical IBM-Celgene, X/Y/Z and Austin Fund examples are used as textbook exercises, not current investment recommendations. CAPM and correlation calculations assume the simplified models and stated conditions.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"question-bank-wrap hard-wrap\" id=\"l5-hard\"><summary><div><strong>Difficult mode - 30 questions</strong><small>18 Bloom-graded questions (3 per level) and 12 marked extension questions. Answers hidden.</small></div><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><p class=\"hard-intro\">Questions climb Bloom&#39;s taxonomy from <b>Remember</b> to <b>Create</b>. The <b>extension</b> questions go beyond the slides (industry practice, CFA-style, case competitions, Master&#39;s, PhD and postdoc level) but can be reasoned out from the chapter&#39;s principles.</p><div class=\"action-row\"><button class=\"primary-btn\" data-quiz=\"5\" data-quiz-mode=\"difficult\">Start Chapter 5 difficult mode</button><button class=\"secondary-btn\" data-quiz=\"5\" data-quiz-mode=\"extensions\">Extensions only</button></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-1\">Remember</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D1</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-1\">Remember</span></div><p>How does the chapter define an <strong>efficient portfolio</strong>?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The portfolio with the highest possible return, whatever the risk.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The portfolio that provides the highest return for a given level of risk.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The portfolio with the largest number of securities.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The portfolio with zero standard deviation.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Efficiency is about the trade-off: highest return for a given risk (equivalently, lowest risk for a given return). A ignores risk, C confuses count with quality, and D is only possible in special cases.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D2</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-1\">Remember</span></div><p>What is the beta of the market portfolio?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 1.0</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 0.5</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 0</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 2.0</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Beta measures sensitivity to the market benchmark, so the market's own beta is 1.0. A beta of 0 means no linear sensitivity to the market.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D3</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-1\">Remember</span></div><p>Which risk can be reduced or eliminated through diversification?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Unsystematic (diversifiable, firm-specific) risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Systematic (nondiversifiable) risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Inflation risk affecting all firms</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> War and political risk</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Firm-specific events such as lawsuits and strikes can be diluted by holding many securities. Systematic risk arises from forces that affect similar investments broadly, so diversification cannot remove it.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D4</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span></div><p>Why is the standard deviation of a portfolio generally <strong>not</strong> the weighted average of its assets' standard deviations?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Because standard deviations cannot be averaged by law.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Because standard deviation is always larger for portfolios.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Because weights do not affect risk.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Because portfolio risk also depends on the correlations between the assets: imperfect co-movement lets fluctuations partly offset.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>The covariance term 2w<sub>A</sub>w<sub>B</sub>σ<sub>A</sub>σ<sub>B</sub>ρ explains it. Only when ρ = +1 does the portfolio σ equal the weighted average.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D5</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span></div><p>Which statement best contrasts beta and standard deviation?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> They measure exactly the same thing.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Both measure only firm-specific risk.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Beta measures total volatility; standard deviation measures market sensitivity.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Standard deviation measures total volatility; beta measures sensitivity to market movements (systematic risk).</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>A stock driven by company-specific news may have a high σ but a low β. Neither replaces the other for every purpose.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D6</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span></div><p>Why does CAPM reward only systematic risk?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Because unsystematic risk does not exist.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Because CAPM was designed for bonds only.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Because systematic risk is always smaller.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Because investors can cheaply diversify away firm-specific risk, so the market has no reason to compensate them for holding it.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>More total risk does not automatically justify a higher CAPM return. What matters is the asset's contribution to market-related risk (an assumption-driven theoretical result).</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D7</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span></div><p>A portfolio is 40% in an asset with E(R) = 10% and 60% in an asset with E(R) = 5%. What is the portfolio's expected return?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 7.5%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 6.0%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 7.0%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 15.0%</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>E(R<sub>p</sub>) = 0.40(10%) + 0.60(5%) = 4% + 3% = <strong>7%</strong>. A is the simple average; D is the sum.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D8</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span></div><p>R<sub>f</sub> = 5%, E(R<sub>m</sub>) = 11% and β = 1.2. What is the CAPM required return?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 12.2%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 18.2%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 13.2%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 7.2%</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>R = 5% + 1.2 x (11% - 5%) = 5% + 7.2% = <strong>12.2%</strong>. B wrongly multiplies beta by the market return; D adds only the premium part.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D9</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span></div><p>A portfolio holds 50% in a stock with β = 1.2, 30% with β = 0.8 and 20% with β = 1.5. What is the portfolio beta?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 1.14</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 1.00</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 1.17</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 1.50</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>β<sub>p</sub> = 0.5(1.2) + 0.3(0.8) + 0.2(1.5) = 0.60 + 0.24 + 0.30 = <strong>1.14</strong>. Portfolio beta is the weighted average of the betas.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D10</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span></div><p>Two assets have σ = 20% and 10% and are held 50/50. If they are uncorrelated (ρ = 0), what is the portfolio standard deviation?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 15.00%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 8.66%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 11.18%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 5.00%</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Variance = 0.25(0.04) + 0.25(0.01) = 0.0125; σ<sub>p</sub> = √0.0125 ≈ <strong>11.18%</strong>. A is the weighted average (ρ = +1); B is ρ = -0.5; D is ρ = -1.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D11</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span></div><p>A stock has σ = 25%, a correlation of 0.6 with the market, and the market has σ = 15%. What is its beta?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 0.40</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 0.60</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 1.67</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 1.00</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>β = ρ(σ<sub>i</sub> / σ<sub>m</sub>) = 0.6 x (25 / 15) = <strong>1.0</strong>. You cannot get beta from the stock's σ alone.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D12</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span></div><p>Stock A has β = 0.5 and σ = 45%. Stock B has β = 1.5 and σ = 30%. Which statement is correct?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> A is safer on every measure.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> B has both higher beta and higher total volatility.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> A has lower market sensitivity but higher total volatility; neither is unconditionally safer.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Beta and σ must always rank assets identically.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Beta captures systematic exposure; σ captures total variation including firm-specific risk. A low beta does not make a stock low-volatility overall.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D13</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span></div><p>A stock with β = 1.5 is expected by an analyst to return 15%. R<sub>f</sub> = 4% and E(R<sub>m</sub>) = 10%. Evaluate.</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> It is overvalued: below the SML.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Required return is 13%, so expected alpha is +2%: above the SML, potentially undervalued, but only as reliable as the forecast.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> It is fairly priced exactly on the SML.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> CAPM cannot be applied with β above 1.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Required = 4% + 1.5(6%) = 13%. Positive alpha is not proof of a bargain; it may reflect an inaccurate forecast or an inadequate model.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D14</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span></div><p>Evaluate: \"Adding more stocks to a portfolio always reduces risk.\"</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Incorrect: a highly correlated additional stock adds little diversification; what matters is whether the new asset reduces covariance or concentration.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Correct.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Correct, but only for stocks above beta 1.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Incorrect, because adding stocks always increases risk.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Number of assets ≠ quality of diversification. Forty bank stocks are still one big bet on banking.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D15</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span></div><p>Evaluate: \"I'll choose a portfolio below the efficient frontier because it feels safer.\"</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Sensible: feelings determine efficiency.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Flawed: a dominated portfolio offers the same risk for less return; a cautious investor should pick a lower-risk point on the frontier instead.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Correct, because the frontier is always riskier.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Correct, because portfolios below the frontier have zero risk.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Comfort and efficiency are different things. Risk preference selects among efficient portfolios; it is not a reason to hold an inefficient one.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D16</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span></div><p>Asset A has σ = 30% and asset B has σ = 10%, with ρ = -1. Which weights make the portfolio riskless?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 50% A, 50% B</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 75% A, 25% B</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 25% A, 75% B</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 10% A, 90% B</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Set w<sub>A</sub>σ<sub>A</sub> = w<sub>B</sub>σ<sub>B</sub>: w<sub>A</sub> x 30 = (1 - w<sub>A</sub>) x 10, so w<sub>A</sub> = 10 / 40 = <strong>25%</strong>. Check: 0.25 x 30 = 7.5 = 0.75 x 10.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D17</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span></div><p>Stock X has β = 1.4 and Stock Y has β = 0.6. What weight in X gives a two-stock portfolio with β = 1.0?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 25%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 60%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 50%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 75%</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>w(1.4) + (1 - w)(0.6) = 1.0 → 0.8w = 0.4 → <strong>w = 0.5</strong>. Beta is linear in the weights.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D18</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span></div><p>Which sequence best constructs a portfolio for an investor, per the chapter's reconciliation of traditional and modern approaches?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Buy the ten highest-return stocks.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Copy the most popular fund.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Decide how much risk you can bear, diversify across securities and industries, check correlations, use portfolio beta to hold risk at an acceptable level, and evaluate alternatives for the best return at that risk.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Hold only familiar domestic companies for comfort.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>This reproduces the chapter's policy: traditional diversification and judgment plus MPT's statistical discipline (correlations and beta).</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension questions</span><small>beyond the notes, lowest Bloom level first</small></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X2</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Industry</span></div><p>Why do index-fund providers emphasise holding the whole market rather than a few stocks, in terms of the chapter's risk decomposition?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> It eliminates all risk.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> It removes the need for beta.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> It guarantees returns above the market.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> A broad portfolio removes most firm-specific risk at low cost, leaving mainly systematic risk, the part the market compensates.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>The diversification curve (Figure 5.8) flattens at the systematic-risk floor. Holding more securities beyond that gives diminishing benefit.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X1</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Industry</span></div><p>R<sub>f</sub> = 4% and E(R<sub>m</sub>) = 10%. What does CAPM require from an asset with β = -0.5, and why is that sensible?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 7%, because all assets need more than the market.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 4%, because beta never changes required return.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> -4%, because it must lose money.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 1%: a negative-beta asset tends to rise when the market falls, so it acts as a hedge and investors accept less return for it.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>4% + (-0.5)(6%) = 1%. Asset value inside a portfolio depends on how it co-moves with everything else, not only on its own expected return.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X3</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · CFA</span></div><p>w<sub>A</sub> = 0.6, w<sub>B</sub> = 0.4, σ<sub>A</sub> = 15%, σ<sub>B</sub> = 25%, ρ = 0.2. What is σ<sub>p</sub>?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 19.0%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 14.7%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 12.0%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 21.7%</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Variance = 0.36(0.0225) + 0.16(0.0625) + 2(0.6)(0.4)(0.15)(0.25)(0.2) = 0.0081 + 0.0100 + 0.0036 = 0.0217; σ<sub>p</sub> = √0.0217 ≈ <strong>14.7%</strong>. A is the weighted average of the σs.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X4</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · CFA</span></div><p>A security has E(R) = 9%, β = 0.7, R<sub>f</sub> = 4% and E(R<sub>m</sub>) = 10%. What is its expected alpha?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> -0.8%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 0%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> +5.0%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> +0.8%</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Required = 4% + 0.7(6%) = 8.2%. Alpha = 9% - 8.2% = <strong>+0.8%</strong>: above the SML.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X5</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Case competition</span></div><p>During a global crisis the correlation between two assets (σ = 20% and 10%, 50/50) rises from 0 to 0.8. Approximately what happens to σ<sub>p</sub>?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> It falls from 11.2% to 8%.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> It becomes 5%.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> It stays at 11.2%.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> It rises from about 11.2% to about 14.3%.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Variance = 0.0125 + 2(0.25)(0.2)(0.1)ρ = 0.0125 + 0.01ρ. At ρ = 0.8: 0.0205, so σ<sub>p</sub> ≈ 14.3%. Historical low correlation is not guaranteed future low correlation.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X7</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Master's</span></div><p>Why can estimated betas be unstable?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> They are estimated from historical returns over a particular window and benchmark, so changes in the firm, the market or the estimation period alter the estimate.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Betas are set by regulators each quarter.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Beta is always exactly 1.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Beta depends only on dividends.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Beta describes a linear relationship estimated from past data; it is not a mechanical multiplier. This limits how confidently SML-based conclusions can be drawn.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X9</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · PhD</span></div><p>A stock's firm-specific risk doubles, but its covariance with the market is unchanged. What does CAPM predict for its required return?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> It doubles.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> It is unchanged: beta depends on covariance with the market, and unsystematic risk is not priced.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> It falls to the risk-free rate.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> It becomes negative.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>β = Cov(R<sub>i</sub>, R<sub>m</sub>) / Var(R<sub>m</sub>). Unchanged covariance means unchanged beta, so the same required return. Investors could diversify the extra risk away.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X6</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Case competition</span></div><p>Portfolios A, B and C have expected alphas of +0.8%, +1.4% and +1.0%, and standard deviations of 12%, 18% and 28%. Evaluate \"Portfolio B must be chosen because it has the highest alpha.\"</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Correct.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Incomplete: the choice also depends on risk tolerance, confidence in the forecasts, feasibility and total and systematic risk.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Incorrect, because alpha is meaningless.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Correct, because alpha equals return.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Alpha is one input. A cautious investor may prefer A (lowest σ and beta) despite a smaller alpha.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X8</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Master's</span></div><p>Portfolio W (β = 0.91) is less market-sensitive than V (β = 1.195). Evaluate: \"W therefore has lower total standard deviation.\"</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Correct.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Correct, because beta equals σ.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Not implied: beta captures only market-related sensitivity; W could still have higher total volatility from firm-specific risk or concentration.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Incorrect, because W has a higher beta.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Beta alone cannot reveal total σ or expected return. The chapter stresses that the two measure different dimensions.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X10</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · PhD</span></div><p>An optimiser built from historical estimates recommends a very concentrated \"efficient\" portfolio. What is the best critical response?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Accept it; mathematics is always right.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Recognise estimation error: small errors in expected returns and correlations can swing optimal weights, so test robustness, add constraints and compare with simple diversified alternatives.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Reject MPT entirely.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Invest only in the highest-return asset.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>The chapter warns that MPT depends on statistical estimates, so a mathematically optimal portfolio can perform poorly in reality if the estimates are poor.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X11</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Postdoc</span></div><p>A manager reports positive alpha against CAPM for ten years. Which interpretation is most defensible?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Consistent with skill, but also with an inadequate pricing model, omitted risk factors, mismeasured beta or luck; further tests are needed.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Proof of superior skill.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Proof that markets are inefficient.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Meaningless because alpha is always zero.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>The notes caution that positive alpha could reflect inaccurate expected-return estimates or an inadequate pricing model. It is a joint test of the manager and the model.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X12</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Postdoc</span></div><p>Which exam question best separates a student who memorised the SML from one who understands what it says?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> \"Two stocks each expect 11%. Stock P has β = 0.5 and Stock Q has β = 1.5; with R<sub>f</sub> = 4% and E(R<sub>m</sub>) = 10%, decide for each whether it is above, on or below the SML, then explain what could make your conclusion wrong.\"</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> \"State the CAPM equation.\"</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> \"What does SML stand for?\"</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> \"Draw the SML from memory.\"</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>P requires 7% (alpha +4%, above) and Q requires 13% (alpha -2%, below). The \"what could be wrong\" part requires evaluating forecasts, beta estimates and the model's assumptions.</p></div></details></div></details></div></div></details>");
